A||||2||theogre||TIP: 4 cyl engine mount.||||4||
Z||000000||theogre||04-08-2001||06:33 PM||||For those of you who haven't checked them... you should. and soon.<P>Mine was completely shot. The engine bracket was sitting directly on the big rivit that goes thru the mount near the top. (I knew it was bad, but I didn't know how bad untill it was out.) For all intents it may as well have been sitting directly on the frame.<P>I found it is easiest to remove the one top mount bolt with a 15mm flare nut wrench. You might get it with a regular box end if it's not horibly tight. (The wrench won't go on the nut all the way because of the braket shap. The 6 point flare wrench gets a better grip.)<P>Before you can lift the motor off the mount, you have to disconect the dog bone.<P>The top nuts are easiest to reach thru the wheel well. I tried it from the bottom. Didn't work. you can't get a good angle to turn the wrench. if the nuts are real tight they probably aren't comming off without the wheel off.<P>I still have a Polly bushing dog bone to the new mount didn't eliminate all the vibration but it seems to have reduced it quite a bit. One of these days I will convert to the old 2 mount tranny setup on my automatic. then I should kill nearly all the engine shake. This single tranny mount setup sucks...<P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I>||||reg||4
Z||000001||Bob Englert||04-08-2001||07:49 PM||||I have had problems with excess engine movement on my daughter's 87 coupe with automatic trans and the terrible single transmission mount system.  We had a poly dogbone on for a while but the vibration was just too much at idle in drive.  I put a new OEM dogbone back on.<P>The movement of the engine would cause the rubber to bulge out significantly from the casing of the dogbone.  Since the inner sleeve is longer than the dogbone casing, the rubber has a lot of movement when it is pushed and pulled.<P>I took the dogbone to my local hardware store and found large washers that would fit over the inner sleeve and cover the rubber sides of the bushing.  With a washer covering all four sides of the bushings in the dogbobe, the rubber could not deflect out of the body of the bushing nearly as much.  The washers acted as spacers to take up the excess space between the rubber portion of the bushing and the mounting brackets.<P>Megan's car seems to have noticably less engine movement during normal driving without the heavy vibration of the poly nount.  I am hoping that by constraining the rubber, the dogbone will last longer than they normally do.<P>You 4 cyl guys might want to give this a try.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||04-08-2001||08:33 PM||||My wife's 87 coupe has the same ridiculous single auto tranny mount. When the engine finally gave up the ghost and it was out I grabbed the mounts and brackets from a V6 auto tranny and replaced them. It makes more sense but I also feel just a bit (tiny bit) more vibration since the powertrain is more stabilized. I think it was worth it though.<P>I can vouch for the engine mounts. Though Ogre didn't say if it was a V6 or 4, Ive seen an 87 coupe with nothing left of the engine mount actually crack and break the dog bone bracket on the engine side. The air cleaner was dented because it would slam up against the firewall on the trunk side anytime it was put into gear! Without the dog bone hooked up it was obscene how easily the engine could be pushed from side to side! <P>They might be expensive but don't let the 4 cylinder engine mount go completely out!||||reg||4
